  "live_records": {
    "q-129769": {
      "dom_id": "question-129769",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Find the value of ${f}^{′}(0).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{′}(0)\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that ${f}^{′}(0)$ is just the slope of the tangent line to the graph of $y=f(x)$ at the point where $x=0.$\n\nNotice that our tangent line passes through the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(4,0)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(0,2).$ Therefore, we can compute its slope as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{2-0}{0-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{-4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(0)=-\\frac{1}{2}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
