      "problem": "Use the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ from part 2 to find the fourth-degree Taylor approximation of $g(x)$ about $x=0.$ Find $g(1)$ using this approximation.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $g(x)\\,≈\\,2x-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}\\,+\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${x}^{3}$\n$+$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${x}^{4}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $g(1)\\,≈\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-3}}",
      "worked_solution": "We have that\n\n$$\nf(x)≈{P}_{3}(x)=2-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate $g(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ by integrating the above polynomial term-by-term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ≈{\\int }_{0}^{x}(2-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{3})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[2t-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}+\\frac{1}{12}{t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{24}{t}^{4}]}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =[2x-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{12}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{4}]-0 \\\\  & =2x-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{12}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $g(1)$ using the approximation above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(1) & ≈2(1)-\\frac{1}{4}(1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{12}(1)^{3}-\\frac{1}{24}(1)^{4} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{12}-\\frac{1}{24} \\\\  & =\\frac{43}{24}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
